    "book_chapter": "Chapter 2: UVM Core Utilities",
    "full_text": "# 01. UVM Core Utilities & Automation Methods\n\nThe **UVM Core Utilities** (defined in IEEE 1800.2 and UVM 1.2) provide the foundation for object modeling, data operations, and transaction manipulation. Every transaction and configuration object in UVM derives from `uvm_object`.\n\nIn *Practical UVM: Step by Step* by Srivatsa Vasudevan, Chapter 2 introduces the essential mechanisms required to manipulate data objects safely without writing repetitive boilerplate code:\n\n### Core Object Operations:\n1. **Copying (`copy()` & `do_copy()`)**: Performing deep vs. shallow copies of verification transactions.\n2. **Comparison (`compare()` & `do_compare()`)**: Field-by-field and policy-driven object matching.\n3. **Printing (`print()`, `sprint()`, `do_print()`)**: Table, tree, and line printing via `uvm_printer`.\n4. **Packing & Unpacking (`pack()`, `unpack()`)**: Serialization of high-level object fields into packed bitstreams.\n5. **Cloning (`clone()`)**: Allocating and duplicating objects in a single polymorphic call.\n6. **Factory Registration**: Using `` `uvm_object_utils `` and `` `uvm_field_* `` macros for automated method generation.\n\n---\n### Code Examples from the Book:\n- **`copy_ex.sv`**: Demonstrates deep copy of transaction objects containing nested data.\n- **`compare_ex.sv`**: Shows how `uvm_comparer` evaluates matching and mismatching transactions.\n- **`print.sv`**: Demonstrates UVM table printers with custom radix (HEX, DEC, BIN).\n- **`new_packer.sv`**: Demonstrates bitstream serialization for physical bus driving.\n- **`completed_class.sv`**: Complete reference implementation of a production-grade `uvm_object`.\n",
    "author_notes": "Note: \nThere were some typos in the code for the uvm_utilities in the BOOK print COPY.\nThese were actually  fixed in the code. The wrong files got picked up.\n\n\n------------------------------------\n\nThe examples for Part 1 of the book are in the subdirectories below this directory.\nThere are 3 makefiles in the run directory. One for each simulator. \n\nPlease note that I have not provided log files for the other two simulators in the run directory for a number of reasons. \nThe makefiles for the Questa and IUS simulators were provided by Srinivasan Venkataraman of CVC Bangalore.  srini@cvcblr.com\nI am grateful to him for making the contribution. \n\nThe make files for  the VCS simulator have been validated and provided in the run directory along with log files. \n\nI appeal to the community at large to run these examples, make any necessary tweaks for the other simulators and share thier log files.\nThe contribution of the log files will be gratefully appreciated.\n\n\n\nPlease note: \nFor IUS and Questa:\nYou may need to set the environment variable UVM_HOME to an area where you have the UVM tarball downloaded.  That is NOT in the makefile\nThe simulator may provide a setting and I invite you to check on that in the documentation. \nIf you are not sure, you can download the tarball from: http://www.accellera.org/images/downloads/standards/uvm/uvm-1.2.tar.gz\nexplode it and set UVM_HOME to the location of the src/ subdirectory.\n\nFor the VCS simulator, it is set to the inbuilt copy of UVM bundled with VCS. \n\nPlease let me know of any problems with these files so that I can correct them.\nif anything is wrong with these instructions for Questa and IUS, and you figure it out, please let me know what the changes are and I will incorporate them\ninto the main repository.\n\nThanks!\nSrivatsa \nJuly 2018\n\n\n\n\n\nThere were a few Minor errors in the listings in Chapter 2 during initial check in.\nI have uploaded the correct sources for these files. Please update your repository.\n\nNote that I have only provided run scripts for copy and compare. The others should be self explanatory, \nand should give you a chance to get familar with your compiler options -:)\n\n\nThe names of the following files  are self explanatory. They should match the listings in the book.\nlisting_2.1.sv\nlisting_2.10\nlisting_2.10.sv\nlisting_2.2.sv\n\nI could have renamed the files which are listed below. However, some of them are a combination of several others to make it easy to compile.  \nSo I chose to leave them the way they are. If you have any suggestions on how to do this cleanly, let me know and I will update GitHub.\n\n\nclone.sv      -- Listing 2.6\ncompare_ex.sv  -- This is Listing 2.7\ncopy.sv        -- This is a combination of Listing 2.1, listing 2.2 and Listing 2.4.\ncopy_ex.sv     -- This  is Listing 2.5 with Listing 2.1 and Listing 2.2 so that it can be run..\ncopy_obj.sv    --- This  is Listing 2.5 with Listing 2.1 and Listing 2.2 so that it can be run..\nnew_packer.sv  -- Listing 2.11\ncompare2.sv    -- This is a complete example for the compare operation\nprint.sv -- Listing 2.9\ncompleted_class.sv   -- Listing 2.12\n\nIf you see any problems, I would appreciate a quick note so that I can fix it. The database is now over 7K files, and \nAny bugs are most likely are a result of copy-paste/move errors that I may have made inadverdently.\n\nThank you\nSrivatsa\n",
