    "book_chapter": "Part 2: Putting It All Together",
    "full_text": "# 02. Putting It All Together: Complete Wishbone UVM Environment\n\nIn Part 2 of *Practical UVM: Step by Step*, the author constructs a complete, industry-standard layered testbench based on the **WISHBONE interconnect protocol** (chosen for its well-defined handshakes and standard open architecture).\n\n### Structural Architecture:\n- **DUT (`dut2.sv`)**: Dummy Wishbone memory slave responding to bus master cycles with `ACK_O` assertion.\n- **Interfaces (`wb_master_if.sv`, `wb_slave_if.sv`)**: SystemVerilog interfaces bundling clock, reset, address (`ADR_O`), data (`DAT_O`/`DAT_I`), strobe (`STB_O`), and cycle (`CYC_O`) lines.\n- **Transaction Object (`wb_transaction.sv`)**: Encapsulates Wishbone bus cycles (READ/WRITE, address, data payload, tag bits).\n- **Master Agent (`wb_master_agent.sv`)**: Active agent encapsulating `wb_master` driver, `wb_master_seqr` sequencer, and `wb_master_mon` monitor.\n- **Slave Agent (`wb_slave_agent.sv`)**: Passive/active slave agent monitoring bus cycles and generating slave acknowledge responses.\n- **Coverage & Scoreboard (`wb_env_cov.sv`)**: Functional coverage collecting cross-coverage on address spaces and transaction types.\n- **Top Environment (`wb_env_env.sv`)**: Container connecting master and slave agents to analysis ports and scoreboard.\n- **Testcase (`wb_env_test.sv`)**: Instantiates environment, sets virtual interfaces via `uvm_config_db`, and starts the master sequence library.\n",
    "author_notes": "This example is the main example upon which all the files are patterned. \nSo I have marked all the files in this example as being important.\n\n\n\n*************************************************************\nSTEPS TO COMPILE AND RUN EXAMPLE\n*************************************************************\n\nNote: \n  1) To include the UVM1.2 inbuilt source from VCS installation:\n     Inclusion of UVM_HOME can be replaced by -ntb_opts uvm command in Makefile\n  2) To use the UVM source other than VCS installation directory:\n     Set environment variable VCS_UVM_HOME to the uvm-1.2 installation directory\n\n*************************************************************\nDirectory Structure of environment generated by UVMGEN\n*************************************************************\n-EXAMPLE\n  \t|\n       -README.md ( This file)\n       |\n       -hdl/        ---> includes top module and inclusion of all environment files.\n       |\n       | - wb_env_top.sv  -- Top level file that instantiates everything\n       | - dut.v     -- Dummy DUT\n       |\n       -env/        ---> includes environment related files (environment, ralenv)\n       |  \n       | - wb_slave_agent.sv  -- top level slave agent file\n       | - wb_master_agent.sv -- top level master agent file\n       | - wb_env_env.sv      -- the complete environment\n       |\n       -include/\n       |\n       -src/        ---> includes Transaction, configurations, driver, monitor, master/slave subenv, scoreboard, coverage related files.\n       |  \n       | - wb_slave.sv     --- Slave driver\n       | - wb_slave_mon.sv  -- Slave monitor\n       | - wb_slave_if.sv   -- slave interface\n       | - wb_transaction.sv  -- transaction class\n       | - wb_slave_seqr.sv   -- Slave Sequencer. This is not used in this example\n       | - wb_master.sv     -- master driver\n       | - wb_master_seqr.sv  -- master sequencer\n       | - wb_master_agent_sequence_library.sv -- All the sequences for the master are HERE\n       | - wb_master_if.sv  -- master interface\n       | - wb_master_mon.sv  -- master monitor \n       | - wb_env_cov.sv   -- coverage\n       | - wb_env_cfg.sv   -- env config class as an example. Not used much in the example\n       |\n       -tests/      ---> includes testbench module and testcases \n       |\n       | - wb_env_tb_mod.sv \n       | - wb_env_test.sv --- This is the actual test you need to look at \n       |\n       -run/        ---> Makefile\n       | - Makefile   \n       | - simv.log -- run log file\n       | - vcs.log  -- compile log file\n\n\n\n\n\n",
